a computer vision project.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.510
And it's been crazy
the amount of progress

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.230
that we've seen in the field.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:28.290
So this is the kind of images
that we were able to generate.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.830
So it's back in 2014.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:37.390
So it was mostly low resolution
black and white pictures.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.150
So for instance, here there
some digits or human faces.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.470
And as you all
know, nowadays we're

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.870
able to generate high
resolution colored images.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:56.710
So for instance those ones
that I generated with ChatGPT.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.190
And the goal of this
class is to understand

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.950
how this image
generation models work

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.570
and what makes
them work so well.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:13.350
So in particular, this
class has two main goals.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.430
So the first goal is to
understand the paradigms

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.110
behind how we can
generate these images.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:23.030
And the second one
is to understand

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:29.790
how the underlying models
are trained and evaluated.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.230
So in case you're
here in this room

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.470
and still wondering if
this class is good for you,

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.850
I would say that
this class is good

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.990
if you have, in general, an
interest in understanding how

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.310
image generation models work.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.270
And in particular,
if this is something

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:48.850
that you wanted to
do as a career goal,

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:53.350
if you want to go in the
field of research to do

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.260
peer research
scientists to research

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.580
how to generate images
in a better way.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.360
Or if you are
yourself interested,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.900
to do that on your personal
time as a personal project,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.780
or just if you're here
out of pure curiosity.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.340
With that being said, I
want to give a warning.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.300
So this class actually
has a pretty strong set

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.900
of prerequisites, and
I'm not going to hide it.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.260
So this slide is just
there to enumerate

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.780
the kinds of
prerequisites that I

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.380
think are good to have just
so that you're not surprised.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.740
So first of all, in case
you have some background

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.306
in linear algebra,
for instance, if you

00:03:38.306 --> 00:03:40.840
know what a vector
is, what a matrix is,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.220
how you do operations,
what is the gradient, what

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.500
is the divergence of a vector
field, that would be great.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.210
If you don't everything,
that's OK too.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.090
So in this class, we will not
assume that you know everything,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.650
but if you have at
least some foundation,

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.810
it's quite important to have it.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.370
So linear algebra is one.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.530
Second one that's quite
important is probability theory.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:17.130
So in particular Bayes'
rule, conditional marginal

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.130
probabilities.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:20.670
What is an expectation?

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.370
What is the covariance
matrix, and what

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.370
is the Gaussian distribution?

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.490
Again, if you don't everything
it's fine, but at least

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.330
most of it or at least
if you've heard of it.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.010
Then you have
differential equations.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:40.510
So here we'll see how we
can use ODEs and SDEs.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.210
So ordinary
differential equations

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:47.170
and stochastic differential
equations and how they matter,

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.250
and how we solve them.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:54.680
And then last but not least,
there's some basics of ML.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.600
So if you know how you train
a model, just in general using

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.900
a neural network, how you do
training, how you do inference,

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.360
that would be great.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.520
But again, we will try
to review these concepts

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.020
in a non-exhaustive way
in an intuitive way.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.440
But yeah, just knowing those,
I think, would be great.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.720
So that's one Warning.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.440
The second thing that I
will say is that this field

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.440
is very technical in general.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:31.240
So if you read a computer
vision image generation paper,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.880
you will see that there are
formulas all over the place.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.440
So the goal of
this class will not

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.120
be to go into all
of these details.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.880
So we will try to strike a
balance between not shying away

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.640
from the math, just
understanding what

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.720
are the important formulas,
but also at the same time,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.920
just understand
why they're there.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.220
So we'll try to make a
big emphasis on intuition.

