00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.880
OK, an important note.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:13.080
So I mentioned that
the papers in the field

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:18.800
are crazy in terms of
technicality and math formulas,

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.880
the same goes for conventions.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:25.240
So you will see that
from paper to paper,

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:30.000
things are noted differently,
and it can be very confusing.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.840
So for instance, you will see
there is a time t that sometimes

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.480
is between 0 and 1,
sometimes it's between 0

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.990
and T, sometimes
it's in reverse,

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:47.270
sometimes a epsilon is not
a Z or X0 or X1, et cetera.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:53.230
So in this class, what we will
try to do is three things.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:56.750
First is we will always try
to rely on the most commonly

00:11:56.750 --> 00:12:00.970
used notations, so that when you
go and refer to those papers,

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.630
you're not lost.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:07.790
The second one is we will try
to stay consistent as much as

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:08.790
possible.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:12.510
So if we take a notation,
we'll try to keep it

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:15.070
so that you're not confused.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:20.050
And if we don't keep it, we'll
just make sure to call it out.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:24.990
OK.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.430
Great.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:31.750
So as I mentioned this class
is all about understanding how

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:34.670
image generation models work.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:37.790
So here you have a
typical setup where

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:42.230
you have let's say a
prompt where you specify

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:44.030
what you want to generate.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:46.970
So here I want to generate
a Teddy bear reading a book.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:49.350
And I put that in some model.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:53.110
And then the model
generates an image.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:56.670
So this is what
we want to cover.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:13:04.130
But as easy as it seems,
it's actually quite complex.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.350
There's a lot of things
moving pieces everywhere.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.190
And I'm going to call
out the main moving

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:12.550
pieces that we'll cover.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:15.750
So the first one
is if you suppose

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:22.430
that you have some model, how do
you go about generating images?

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:26.470
What is the process
of generating images?

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:29.010
So in the text
world is quite easy.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:33.110
The most common paradigm
is you generate one token

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:36.580
at a time, because that's
also what people talk

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:39.500
and how people write,
so it's quite intuitive,

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:43.860
but in the image world,
it's not straightforward.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:50.900
So we will focus lectures
1, 2, and 3 on this part.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:54.280
And we will cover the
main ways of doing it,

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:56.420
which are the most
commonly used today, which

00:13:56.420 --> 00:14:02.280
are diffusion, score
matching, and flow matching.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:11.420
Then we will look at how we can
condition our model to generate

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.860
these images to start with.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.020
So we'll cover
that in lecture 4.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:20.220
So in some sense we
want to guide the model

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.680
to generate something, and
this is what we will cover.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:28.792
We'll also cover how to
represent images in a space that

00:14:28.792 --> 00:14:30.000
quote unquote, "makes sense."

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:39.410
So lecture 5 is going to be
about the model architecture.

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:43.210
So we'll take a look
at the backbones that

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.050
are most commonly used today.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:48.530
So back in the day you
may have heard of u-net

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:50.570
as being something
that was used.

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.370
But there's currently a
convergence of architectures

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:56.750
more towards transformer
based architectures,

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:59.530
such as the
diffusion transformer

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:02.450
and variants such as
the multimodal diffusion

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.950
transformer, and we will
take a look at those there.

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:11.170
Then lecture 6 will
be around knowing

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.530
how to train those models.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:17.170
So in particular, how
do you have your model

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:21.090
understand how to
generate images

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:26.010
and any post-training approaches
that you may also need.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.850
There's also something that
is quite important, especially

00:15:28.850 --> 00:15:33.520
in the industry, which
is you have your model,

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:36.760
but you want to run
it quicker because you

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.220
have some constraints
in your setup

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:42.560
so we will see also
distillation techniques.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.680
So this will be in lecture 6.

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:51.240
And finally lecture 7 will
be about evaluating images.

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:54.000
Because if you
look at an image--

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.960
I'm not sure about you,
but it's not super obvious

00:15:56.960 --> 00:16:04.120
how you would define an image
as being great or not great.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.880
So you will see that the
field is full of acronyms

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:09.400
when it comes to metrics.

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:13.400
And each of those is there
actually for a reason

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.160
and we'll try to see that.

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:17.080
And then the second
thing that we will see

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:23.480
is this emergence
of multi-modal LLMs

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:26.680
that also understand
images that can also

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:30.000
help us to evaluate them.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.780
So this will be for lecture 7.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:40.280
And today as mentioned, we
will start with the generation

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:41.300
paradigm one.

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:45.880
And in particular, we
will look at diffusion.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:48.880
So who has heard of diffusion.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.200
Yeah, OK so everyone.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:55.960
So we will see beyond the
general intuition of roughly

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:57.360
how that works.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.880
We'll see exactly
how this was defined

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:05.240
and what is making this
objective be tractable

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:07.560
in practice.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.920
So one thing that
I want to call out

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:14.240
is in the first
three lectures, we

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:20.000
will assume that there
is nothing as input.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:23.599
So we have some
distribution of data

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:26.150
that we use as training sets.

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:30.470
And what we want is to generate
images that are like them.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.270
So in mathematical
terms, we want

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:36.830
to have a model that is able
to sample an observation

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:38.630
from the data distribution.

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:43.750
So in a sense we'll only focus
on unconditioned generations

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:46.050
for the first three
lectures including this one.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:50.090
Cool.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:53.230
So speaking of
data distribution.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:57.390
So let's suppose we
have a set of images.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:01.472
So here a long running
example will be Teddy Bears.

00:18:01.472 --> 00:18:03.430
So here let's say we have
a collection of Teddy

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:06.110
Bears as training images.

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:13.190
Our goal is to understand
a way to sample

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:16.550
an observation from that data
distribution that is new.

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:22.990
So what we want to do is to be
able to generate an image that

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.640
is in that data distribution.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:29.220
So here for instance giraffe.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:33.920
And again, just emphasizing
that this is unconditioned.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.900
We're not putting
anything as input.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.740
We just want to
generate a sample that

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:42.860
is following that distribution.

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:45.620
So how do we do this?

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:50.380
So you will see that most if
not all methods out there,

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:53.420
they all have
something in common.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:59.300
They all start their image
generation process from noise.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:00.860
So they start from
noise, and then

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:04.700
they refine the image
to be the final image.

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:10.060
So I want to just spend some
time on why that is the case.

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:12.780
So we'll define what
noise means, by the way

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:15.080
because you will
see the term noise

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:18.540
and then you will see
this grayish image.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:22.890
So you will see that this
noise is actually something

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:26.890
that you can sample from
an easy distribution,

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:29.570
the Gaussian distribution,
spoiler alert.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:33.610
So the first reason why it is
something that we want to do

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:36.570
is because it is easy
to sample from noise.

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:39.530
It is easy to have some noise.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:46.570
The second thing is noise has
some randomness that allows you

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:49.250
to generate different images.

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:52.570
So even if let's say
you have a condition,

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:55.910
if you start from noise
that is different,

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:58.570
let's say from
another generation,

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:02.450
you will be able to end up
with a different end image

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:05.650
just because the starting
point is different.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:10.170
So the second reason it
introduces some randomness.

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:14.330
And then the third one
is, as I mentioned,

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:20.010
noise is basically equal
to a Gaussian distribution,

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:21.850
and that distribution
just happens

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:25.250
to have very nice properties.

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.430
So it simplifies
a lot of the math

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:29.930
and we will see a
little bit how that is

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:31.750
the case in the diffusion case.

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:34.790
Cool.

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:36.770
So intuitively does that
make sense, that noise

00:20:36.770 --> 00:20:39.410
is a good starting point?

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:41.010
Yeah?

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:42.510
OK, cool.

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:47.570
And just to complete that
intuition, in the 3D world,

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:52.770
how sculptors go about
sculpting their sculptors,

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:57.130
they start with some rock.

00:20:57.130 --> 00:21:01.143
And you can think of this
rock as having shapes

00:21:01.143 --> 00:21:02.310
that are a little bit noisy.

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:04.790
It's not something
that you are defining.

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:07.090
It's something
that is just there.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:10.970
And that will be different
from a rock to another.

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:12.970
So one analogy that
I like to think of

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:16.290
is this image generation
process is a little bit

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:22.200
if you were a sculptor and you
wanted to sculpture from rock.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:25.400
So yeah, I will leave you with
that quote from Michelangelo

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:30.600
that I think also
reflects that pretty well.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.320
Cool.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:32.860
Yeah.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.840
So when you say uniforms,
you mean the same thing?

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:05.680
Like let's say blank?

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:08.000
Yeah so the question
is, why do you

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:09.400
want to start from
noise, but not

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:11.220
from something
that is predefined,

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:14.800
let's say a black-- sorry
a white canvas, let's say.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:15.970
So yeah, great question.

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:21.030
So you will see that
what makes the end--

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:24.230
so the generation
process sometimes

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:28.210
involves some randomness
in the generation process,

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:30.310
sometimes it does not.

00:22:30.310 --> 00:22:34.230
The randomness is something that
occurs at the starting points.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:38.030
So if you were to always
start your process

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:43.670
from the same starting point,
given that everything else does

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:47.190
not introduce some
amount of randomness,

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:53.190
you will always end up
at the same endpoint.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:57.830
So in a sense, what you want
is to have some variation

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:01.150
introduced somewhere in
order to not always end up

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:01.930
at the same place.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:03.590
So I'll give you an example.

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:07.030
This Teddy bear reading a book.

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:10.630
If you use a generation
paradigm that does not

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:14.470
have any stochasticity
in its process,

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:18.070
then if you always start
from the same point,

00:23:18.070 --> 00:23:20.623
then you will always end
up at the same endpoint,

00:23:20.623 --> 00:23:22.790
and that's probably not
something that you want just

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:24.670
for diversity purposes.

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:27.310
So that's the intuition.

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:28.450
Yeah, great question.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:34.070
Cool.

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:34.610
OK.

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:41.470
So what we'll focus on for this
lecture is a landmark paper

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:47.590
that I will talk a lot in
depth, that's called DPM.

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:53.990
That was the first that
applying this diffusion paradigm

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:56.990
for images in a successful way.

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:59.150
So what we're going
to do is to go

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:04.070
through what makes this
approach work so well,

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:07.990
and the underlying mechanisms
that lead to the loss

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:10.230
function that we have.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:14.500
So in particular, the
idea that we have here

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:19.940
is what we want is to
generate new images.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:26.100
So what we do have is a
set of training images

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:31.540
and our goal is if we
were to start from noise,

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:35.500
how would we be able to
recover those images?

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:37.780
That's what we want to learn.

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:42.600
Because as I mentioned during
our generation process,

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:44.860
our strategy is to
start from noise

00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:47.860
and end up with a clean image.

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:53.540
So here what we want to do is
to use those training set images

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:58.220
to learn that denoising process.

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:02.620
So in that regard, we
have two processes that

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:04.320
have a very particular name.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:08.780
So the first one is called
the forward process that

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:15.490
amounts to taking an image
from your training set,

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:20.030
and you are actually
corrupting that image.

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:22.370
You're adding some noise.

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:26.050
So here you have
some process that you

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:33.650
define that goes from
some image xt minus 1,

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:40.730
and then produces an image
xt by adding some noise.

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:43.850
So again, this is a
process that you define.

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:48.570
So you know what Q is
in the forward process.

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:54.930
And what you want to do is
to start from that noise

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:58.130
distribution that is
so easy to sample,

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:01.850
and then go through
the reverse process

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:11.800
to actually denoise that
sample back to the clean space.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:16.340
And so what you want
to learn is a model,

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:19.200
that is something we will
characterize with the parameters

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:20.720
theta--

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:29.000
what you want to learn is how
you would go from a noisy image

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:30.500
to a less noisy one.

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:40.760
So you may wonder, OK, so
you're talking about xts,

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:42.380
but we don't what is xt.

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:44.840
It's just a noisy image.

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:47.200
How do you go about
just representing images

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:50.200
in the first place?

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:56.260
So here we will just see one
way of representing images.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:59.680
That is not the way people
usually go about it.

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:02.980
That's something that we'll
go more in depth in lecture 4,

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:06.520
but I just want to tell you
how you would try to represent

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:09.280
images in this framework.

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:15.600
So as you know, an image
is composed of pixels.

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:20.800
So you have a height
and you have a width.

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:23.440
So each pixel is
actually composed

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:28.240
of three values red,
green, and blue,

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:33.600
and this allows you to
cover a number of colors.

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:39.040
So based on that, you
can represent your image

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.760
as a function of
all the pixels that

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:47.320
are composing that image,
where each of these pixels

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.800
contains some value for
each of these colors.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:53.080
So you can think of
each pixel as having

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:57.217
three values, one that
represents the amount of red,

00:27:57.217 --> 00:27:59.300
the other one that represents
the amount of green,

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.508
and the other one that
represents the amount of blue.

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:08.350
So based on that, it is fair
to say that you can represent

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:11.710
images in a vectorial fashion.

00:28:11.710 --> 00:28:16.390
So here the dimensionality
of the image in this example

00:28:16.390 --> 00:28:20.230
would be equal to the
number of pixels times

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:22.630
the number of dimensions
you need to represent

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:24.470
in a pixel, which is 3.

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:27.110
So here you would
have n dimensions

00:28:27.110 --> 00:28:31.710
where n is equal to
number of pixels times 3.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:35.630
So this would be one way
to represent your images.

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:38.350
Of course, this is not
the most clever way

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:42.150
of representing images, and we
will see that more in lecture 4.

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.950
But what I want
you to remember is

00:28:44.950 --> 00:28:47.950
when we talk about
images here, when

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:52.390
we say x0, xt and so on,
what we're talking about

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:55.110
is actually vectors.

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:56.710
And these vectors
are the ones that

00:28:56.710 --> 00:28:58.330
are representing these images.

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:04.540
Make sense?

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:05.480
Cool.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:07.000
So, speaking of vectors.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:10.580
So I told you there is a
prerequisite on probability

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:14.500
theory, so I'm just going
to cover the basics here.

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:18.585
So you may know the common
distribution, that's

00:29:18.585 --> 00:29:19.960
called the Gaussian
distribution,

00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:24.420
normal distribution where you
have some random variable that

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:27.380
is drawn from a
distribution of mean mu

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:30.900
and then variance sigma squared.

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:36.580
Well, you can have the
same thing for vectors.

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:40.300
So I know all these papers
they assume you know that.

00:29:40.300 --> 00:29:41.920
When you read, it's
not super clear,

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:43.820
so just want to emphasize.

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:48.660
You can also have random
variables that are vectors.

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:52.180
And in this case, you
will have a vector that

00:29:52.180 --> 00:29:55.660
is drawn from a
distribution of some mean

00:29:55.660 --> 00:30:00.130
and the mean is a vector
and then some variance,

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:03.950
which is actually represented
by a covariance matrix.

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:10.110
So don't worry if you don't
about the covariance matrix,

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:13.250
because most of the
time we actually

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:17.170
simplify the
covariance matrix to be

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:21.490
a variance times the identity.

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:24.610
And we call this
formulation actually--

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:27.690
so these are like
isotropic Gaussians.

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:30.370
Because what that means
is that the variation

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:35.130
of the data across all
directions is the same.

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:38.250
So most of the time, to
just simplify the math,

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:41.850
what we will assume is that
the covariance is actually

00:30:41.850 --> 00:30:46.370
equal to sigma squared i, which
is the diagonal matrix which

00:30:46.370 --> 00:30:53.090
you see here and not the super
complicated covariance matrix.

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:57.010
So by the way, do you remember
what covariance is roughly?

00:30:57.010 --> 00:30:58.750
So maybe I can just recap.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:00.510
So what is covariance?

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:05.970
So covariance is a
quantity that quantifies

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:11.330
how two random variables
vary in a linear way.

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:13.990
Of course, there is some
mathematical expression,

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:15.910
but this is like the
general intuition.

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:22.610
So speaking of
intuition, when we

00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:29.070
say that a distribution is
isotropic, this is what we mean.

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:34.530
We mean that the variance is
the same across all directions.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:38.970
So you can think of it as
some circle where the color

00:31:38.970 --> 00:31:40.870
represents the density.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:45.850
Yeah.

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:47.170
OK, cool.

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:49.250
And of course, what's
great about Gaussians

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:52.690
is you also have an
analytical expression that's

00:31:52.690 --> 00:31:56.480
quite simple about the
probability density function.

00:31:56.480 --> 00:32:01.200
So I'm just putting this here,
and it's easy to compute.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.200
That's great.

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:07.640
So going back to
what we call noise.

00:32:07.640 --> 00:32:14.000
So here we saw that images
can be represented by vectors.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.920
So when we say we
sample from noise,

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:19.800
we will say here
that we actually

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:25.960
sample from a standard
normal distribution.

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:28.160
So we usually note that epsilon.

00:32:28.160 --> 00:32:32.800
So epsilon will be drawn from a
normal distribution of mean 0.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:37.440
So 0 is actually the
vector, the 0 vector.

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:42.340
And then the covariance will
actually be the identity matrix.

00:32:44.860 --> 00:32:45.360
Yeah.

00:32:58.350 --> 00:32:59.613
It's a great question.

00:32:59.613 --> 00:33:00.530
It's a great question.

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:02.550
So the question is
is the representation

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:03.990
continuous or discrete?

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:07.870
So we talked about pixel
values that were discrete 0, 1,

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:09.730
2 until 255.

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:13.550
But in practice, these are
actually scaled between,

00:33:13.550 --> 00:33:18.170
let's say minus 1 and 1 or some
value that's more of a float.

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:20.770
So you can assume that these
are actually continuous.

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:26.810
But you can assume it's
actually continuous.

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:27.310
Yeah.

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:37.650
So there's always
some assumption.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:40.870
And so here at
least when you say

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:46.150
there is no correlation
between pixels-- so here

00:33:46.150 --> 00:33:49.230
we actually mean the noise.

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.650
So noise we actually
just sample at random.

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:52.730
So yeah, yeah.

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:54.770
It's not true for
actual images, though.

00:33:54.770 --> 00:33:55.670
No, it's not true.

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:56.910
Yeah, yeah.

00:33:56.910 --> 00:33:59.950
Great questions.

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:00.610
OK, cool.

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:03.570
I'm just looking at the time and
I know we have a lot of content,

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:05.790
so I'll just go a
little bit faster.

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:09.409
Just want to know if
the pace so far is good.

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:09.909
Yeah.

00:34:09.909 --> 00:34:10.409
OK.

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:11.870
I don't want to
lose you because I

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:18.389
think this is the first lecture
that is maybe the most difficult

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:20.070
in terms of math.

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:23.230
So it's just kind of sad that
this is the first lecture.

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:26.190
But yeah, let's continue.

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:30.710
So as I mentioned, what we want
to do is to take clean images

00:34:30.710 --> 00:34:32.590
and to corrupt them.

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:37.949
So what we do is we take some
image that maybe we have noised

00:34:37.949 --> 00:34:40.670
and we add some noise.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:44.230
And we actually add
these quantities

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:47.150
in a weighted fashion.

00:34:47.150 --> 00:34:54.940
And these weights are
actually defined by us.

00:34:54.940 --> 00:35:00.300
So if you remember, when we
talked about queue, the process

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:02.900
that we defined for
the forward process,

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:06.580
is actually something
that we define.

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:09.360
So this is how we
know it's images.

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:10.520
So we take an image.

00:35:10.520 --> 00:35:12.398
We take some noise
from a distribution,

00:35:12.398 --> 00:35:13.940
from the normal
distribution and then

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:19.740
we do a weighted average with
some weights that we will see.

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:22.260
And speaking of
those, you will hear

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:27.460
of a recurring term across
all papers, noise schedule.

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:29.620
So the noise
schedule is actually

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:32.820
referring to the
coefficients here that you

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:37.260
use to multiply these values.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.320
So it's a schedule
because at some point,

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:45.140
you may add maybe less noise
than some other points.

00:35:45.140 --> 00:35:47.420
Yeah?

00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:47.920
OK.

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:48.420
Great.

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:53.290
So let's go a little
bit more into the math.

00:35:53.290 --> 00:35:58.850
So we start from x0.

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:02.050
And then the way
we go about adding

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:08.850
noise is we have our forward
process that we've defined

00:36:08.850 --> 00:36:14.730
q which is actually
something we will draw

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:18.210
from a normal distribution.

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:22.830
So we can express the
quantity-- so q of xt,

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:26.650
so the noisier image compared
to the less noisy image

00:36:26.650 --> 00:36:29.730
as a normal
distribution centered

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:34.770
on that less noisy image
with some coefficient

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:37.250
where you add some
noise, which is

00:36:37.250 --> 00:36:41.550
something that is represented by
the covariance matrix beta ti.

00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:46.767
Yeah.

00:36:46.767 --> 00:36:47.600
So what that means--

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:50.800
I'm just going to
rewrite this in a very--

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:52.900
so it's just what that means.

00:36:52.900 --> 00:37:03.280
So it's xt is equal to
1 minus beta xt minus 1

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:09.500
plus square root of
beta and some noise.

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:19.400
So this seemingly math
formula is just nothing else

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:22.720
than just you taking
your less noisy image,

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:24.880
weighting that by
some coefficient,

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:30.040
and then adding some noise
with some coefficient.

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:33.120
Now you may wonder why are
you using these coefficients?

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:36.120
So we will see later in
the class why we do that.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:40.280
But you can see that the
variance of this whole thing

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:43.420
is preserved from
one step to another.

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:47.880
So you will see that this
way of noising images

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:52.640
is called variance
preserving, VP.

00:37:52.640 --> 00:37:54.020
We'll see that more later on.

00:37:57.040 --> 00:38:00.040
OK, cool.

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:02.760
So speaking of the
noise schedule.

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:06.460
So these beta t are
not typically uniform,

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:08.160
they're varying.

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:11.520
And the way they are
defined in the DDPM paper

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:15.480
is they're gradually increased.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:18.840
As in you will start
from your clean image,

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.560
and you will just add
a little bit of noise,

00:38:21.560 --> 00:38:24.740
and then maybe a little bit
more, and again and again.

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:27.880
And then at the end, you
will have much bigger noise.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:29.760
And the reason
why people do that

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:34.560
is because when you're close to
the clean image, what you care

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:37.880
about is to learn
those little details,

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:41.760
but when you're closer to
the noise, what you care more

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:46.430
about learning is the
general shape of the image.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:48.190
So this noise
schedule is actually

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:51.470
quite important in
our ability to learn

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:55.910
and it's just one
way of doing things.

00:38:55.910 --> 00:38:57.110
Yeah.

00:38:57.110 --> 00:38:59.990
And as you can tell,
it's a between 0 and 1,

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:02.790
well, because otherwise you
would have a negative value

00:39:02.790 --> 00:39:06.350
in the square root.

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:06.890
Cool.

00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:07.650
Moving on.

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:14.430
So if you define your
process like this

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:18.390
then it turns out you
don't need to noise

00:39:18.390 --> 00:39:24.950
your images in a sequential way
in order to get the number t1.

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:28.030
You can actually go directly
from your clean image

00:39:28.030 --> 00:39:29.290
to the step number t.

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:36.870
Do you see how
you would do that?

00:39:36.870 --> 00:39:40.740
Maybe it's not super
obvious, but I told you

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:43.700
that Gaussians had
very nice properties.

00:39:43.700 --> 00:39:47.260
So here, epsilon,
the noise is drawn

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:49.620
from a Gaussian distribution.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:53.880
So if you were to
rewrite-- so first of all,

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:57.980
I will just define
alpha t, which

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:04.340
is just equal to 1 minus beta
t is just a reparametrization.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:08.180
So here it will be
1 minus alpha t.

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:14.700
So here what we're
saying is xt minus 1

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:18.460
we can also use the
same formula, which

00:40:18.460 --> 00:40:26.660
will be equal to square root
of alpha t minus 1 xt minus 2

00:40:26.660 --> 00:40:31.540
plus square root of 1
minus alpha t minus 1

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:33.820
and the other Gaussian.

00:40:33.820 --> 00:40:38.530
So these noise are independent.

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:44.370
So if you were to express
xt as a function of xt

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:53.210
minus 2 and the epsilons,
what would you do?

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:57.690
So here I'm just
going to expand xt.

00:40:57.690 --> 00:41:02.010
So here I'm just in parentheses.

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:07.930
So it's equal to alpha t
square root of alpha t.

00:41:07.930 --> 00:41:11.130
So I'm directly going to
multiply this quantity

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:14.050
by the one that we have here.

00:41:14.050 --> 00:41:18.290
So it's square root of
alpha t times square root

00:41:18.290 --> 00:41:36.930
of alpha t minus 1 xt minus 2
plus square root of alpha t.

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:42.510
Square root of 1
minus alpha t minus 1.

00:41:46.330 --> 00:41:48.763
T minus 1 plus.

00:41:48.763 --> 00:41:50.430
So by the way, I'm
just doing some math.

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:53.370
Very basic math.

00:41:53.370 --> 00:42:03.090
Plus square root of 1
minus alpha t epsilon t.

00:42:03.090 --> 00:42:05.690
So far so good?

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:10.510
So here we have our first term.

00:42:10.510 --> 00:42:12.290
No problem.

00:42:12.290 --> 00:42:18.370
Then what we have is a weighted
sum of random variables

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:21.690
drawn from a Gaussian
distribution.

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:25.090
So I told you that these
Gaussian distributions

00:42:25.090 --> 00:42:27.290
had very nice properties.

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:31.810
So it turns out that if you
have two random variables that

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:36.100
are independent and drawn
from a Gaussian distribution,

00:42:36.100 --> 00:42:39.680
so you can really
have this as being

00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:45.120
equal to the standard
deviation of this quantity

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:49.920
times a random variable that
is also drawn from a Gaussian

00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:52.480
distribution.

00:42:52.480 --> 00:42:54.697
So how do you add this variance?

00:42:54.697 --> 00:42:56.280
So you have the
variance of everything

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:59.640
that is equal to the variance
of 1 plus the variance of other.

00:42:59.640 --> 00:43:04.520
So here in terms of
how that looks like--

00:43:04.520 --> 00:43:07.440
so here, you will
have the variance

00:43:07.440 --> 00:43:10.340
that will be equal to alpha t.

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:14.120
So it's basically this
squared plus this squared.

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:18.440
So it's alpha t1 minus--

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:22.760
1 minus alpha t minus
1 plus 1 minus alpha t

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:27.960
which is equal to--
so this one goes away.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:34.050
And then you have 1 minus
alpha t, alpha t minus 1.

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:42.750
So what does that mean?

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:56.230
That means that xt is equal to
square roots of alpha t alpha t

00:43:56.230 --> 00:43:57.470
minus 1.

00:43:57.470 --> 00:44:01.390
So I'm just like
copying what is above.

00:44:01.390 --> 00:44:04.030
Xt minus 2.

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:10.290
Plus the standard
deviation of this one,

00:44:10.290 --> 00:44:18.330
which is square
root of the variance

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:22.550
times a random
variable that is drawn

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:24.490
from the normal distribution.

00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:35.310
And this sketch of a
proof is just a way for us

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:41.270
to prove that you can actually
cascade these things up to 0.

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:43.870
And therefore you can
have an expression

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:49.230
of the noisy image at
step t as a function

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:53.310
of the clean image at step 0.

00:44:53.310 --> 00:44:57.870
And this can be expressed
as a function of alpha bar,

00:44:57.870 --> 00:45:04.910
where alpha bar is equal to
the product of the alpha I's.

00:45:04.910 --> 00:45:08.870
So here alpha t alpha t
minus 1 is just the start.

00:45:08.870 --> 00:45:11.990
But if you continue it's just
the product of all the alpha

00:45:11.990 --> 00:45:12.530
t's.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:18.030
Does that make sense?

00:45:18.030 --> 00:45:22.790
So what I just wrote on the
board is just a way for you

00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:26.730
to be convinced that what
I'm saying is making sense.

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:31.100
So you can express xt
as a function of x0

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:40.100
by just using alpha t bar
instead of this beta t.

00:45:40.100 --> 00:45:41.100
Cool.

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:41.600
So yeah.

00:45:41.600 --> 00:45:43.220
So the derivation
you use the fact

00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:46.740
that the variance of the
sum of independent Gaussians

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:50.140
is just the sum of the
respective variances.

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:54.820
And by the way, there's
many derivations

00:45:54.820 --> 00:45:58.580
we can do in this class, the
problem is we do not have time.

00:45:58.580 --> 00:46:00.100
So here is the
first one, which is

00:46:00.100 --> 00:46:02.760
why I wrote on the blackboard.

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:05.940
But if we do not have
time, we will always

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:10.780
have a little message at the
bottom of the slide that just

00:46:10.780 --> 00:46:14.540
tells you what the trick is.

00:46:14.540 --> 00:46:16.460
And you can safely
assume that if we

00:46:16.460 --> 00:46:20.120
don't derive this
thing in class,

00:46:20.120 --> 00:46:24.060
it will not be asked
during the exams.

00:46:24.060 --> 00:46:26.020
OK.

00:46:26.020 --> 00:46:27.530
Cool.

00:46:27.530 --> 00:46:28.810
Moving on.

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:36.050
So now what we have done is just
defined our forward process,

00:46:36.050 --> 00:46:40.410
and we just also
derived a convenient way

00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:45.810
to noise our image from step
0 to step T without going

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:48.970
through all the steps.

00:46:48.970 --> 00:46:56.050
So what we will do now is
to see how we can actually

00:46:56.050 --> 00:47:01.210
go about determining the process
that denoisers that forest

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:03.090
process.

00:47:03.090 --> 00:47:06.210
So what we want to
is how can we go

00:47:06.210 --> 00:47:15.530
about seeing what p theta
of xt minus 1 given xt is.

00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:21.730
So as I mentioned, what we want
to do is to learn p of theta.

00:47:21.730 --> 00:47:26.847
So in case you have
some experience in ML,

00:47:26.847 --> 00:47:28.680
you will see that a
common trick that people

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:35.120
do is given some training
data what they want to do

00:47:35.120 --> 00:47:40.040
is to maximize the
probability of your model

00:47:40.040 --> 00:47:43.060
seeing the training
data maximized.

00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:47.440
They want to maximize such
a probability with respect

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:49.680
to theta.

00:47:49.680 --> 00:47:53.440
So they want to find the
parameters theta that

00:47:53.440 --> 00:47:58.760
maximize the probability of your
model of seeing your training

00:47:58.760 --> 00:47:59.340
data.

00:48:02.080 --> 00:48:05.380
So in this case training data
is just our training images.

00:48:05.380 --> 00:48:06.960
So it's clean images.

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:10.040
And then our model
here is something

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:13.380
that has p theta in it.

00:48:13.380 --> 00:48:17.880
So it's the probability of
an image happening that we

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:20.000
will see how we can condition.

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:23.360
And what we want to do is to
find the parameters theta that

00:48:23.360 --> 00:48:25.840
do that.

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:30.680
So you will see that whenever
you talk about probabilities,

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:33.880
you will always or
most of the time

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:36.600
talk about log probabilities.

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:39.560
One because the
logarithm function

00:48:39.560 --> 00:48:41.840
has pretty nice properties.

00:48:41.840 --> 00:48:45.120
And then the second
reason is that it's just

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:47.960
computationally more stable,
because probabilities

00:48:47.960 --> 00:48:50.160
are between 0 and 1.

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:53.080
So if you have very,
very small values of p,

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:56.880
then it can be a
little bit unstable.

00:48:56.880 --> 00:49:00.600
So before we
determine that, I just

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:06.400
wanted to go through some
refresher of a concept

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:11.480
in probabilities that is the
joint probability distribution.

00:49:11.480 --> 00:49:17.240
So here if you suppose that you
have two random variables that

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:20.510
are drawn from
two distributions,

00:49:20.510 --> 00:49:27.230
let's say it's x1 and x2, the
random variables, then you

00:49:27.230 --> 00:49:33.870
can compute the joint
probability distribution,

00:49:33.870 --> 00:49:41.270
by taking the probability
density of x1,

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:45.110
and multiplying that by
the conditional probability

00:49:45.110 --> 00:49:47.630
of x2 given x1.

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:49.350
So this is just by
definition, it's

00:49:49.350 --> 00:49:52.550
the probability chain of rule.

00:49:52.550 --> 00:49:59.510
And in practice, when you have a
joint probability distribution,

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:03.030
one way you can represent
that is through a heat map.

00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:08.330
So here you have, let's
say, your two axes.

00:50:08.330 --> 00:50:11.830
One is representing
the one along x1

00:50:11.830 --> 00:50:14.110
and the other one along x2.

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:17.430
And then here the
color that you see here

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:23.560
is the density value of this
joint probability distribution.

00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:33.820
So this is for
instance 1 square.

00:50:33.820 --> 00:50:35.460
And the way you
would go about doing

00:50:35.460 --> 00:50:38.100
this for the conditional
probabilities

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:41.200
you would actually just
look at one column.

00:50:43.780 --> 00:50:46.700
And here the probability
of x2 given x1

00:50:46.700 --> 00:50:52.740
would be the proportion of the
value of the joint probability

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:57.900
distribution in that square
over the sum of all the squares

00:50:57.900 --> 00:51:01.780
for that given value x1.

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:05.460
So far I'm just
recapping what we

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:08.060
know about joint
probability distributions

00:51:08.060 --> 00:51:11.700
and conditional probabilities.

00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:17.010
So what I want to tell
you is a notion of what

00:51:17.010 --> 00:51:24.330
we call marginalization,
which is if you want to obtain

00:51:24.330 --> 00:51:28.530
the probability of just
one random variable,

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:33.650
then what you can do is to
sum the joint probability

00:51:33.650 --> 00:51:37.330
of that random variable and
a second random variable,

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:43.530
and you can sum it along
that second variable.

00:51:43.530 --> 00:51:46.570
And this is called
marginalization.

00:51:46.570 --> 00:51:50.610
Do you all know-- have you all
heard what marginalization is?

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:51.970
Yeah?

00:51:51.970 --> 00:51:52.930
OK, cool.

00:51:52.930 --> 00:51:57.450
So what I'm saying is a
little bit obvious then.

00:51:57.450 --> 00:52:03.490
So in this figure so the
way you would obtain p of x1

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:11.250
is you sum all the squares
relative to x1 again and again

00:52:11.250 --> 00:52:15.450
and again and you can
reconstitute your probability

00:52:15.450 --> 00:52:17.650
distribution.

00:52:17.650 --> 00:52:19.890
So that's the way
you would see this

00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:24.010
from a graphical perspective.

00:52:24.010 --> 00:52:28.330
And so we saw that when we
only have two random variables,

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:34.650
but you can very well do that
when we have T random variables.

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:39.650
And so here we have the
probability chain rule

00:52:39.650 --> 00:52:41.430
like at the top of the slide.

00:52:41.430 --> 00:52:44.150
So you have p of
x1 times p of x2,

00:52:44.150 --> 00:52:51.610
given x1 et cetera times p of
xt given x1 up to xt minus 1.

00:52:51.610 --> 00:52:58.730
So this is just extending
that to a dimension of t.

00:52:58.730 --> 00:53:03.610
And one notation that you
will see very often in papers

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:05.650
is this one.

00:53:05.650 --> 00:53:11.010
So the joint probability
distribution of T variables

00:53:11.010 --> 00:53:17.640
is usually denoted p of x1t.

00:53:17.640 --> 00:53:20.840
So just to save space.

00:53:20.840 --> 00:53:23.120
And the reason why I'm
presenting these slides

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:27.000
is just to tell you that
this is the main notation

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:29.500
that people use and
this is what it means.

00:53:32.060 --> 00:53:32.560
Cool.

00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:35.800
And with that
simplified notation,

00:53:35.800 --> 00:53:42.320
you can just rewrite the
formulas from before as follows.

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:46.560
So now if we were to
come back to the problem

00:53:46.560 --> 00:53:52.120
that we had around finding
parameters theta that

00:53:52.120 --> 00:53:58.040
maximize the probability of our
models seeing the training data.

00:53:58.040 --> 00:54:03.080
Here, what we want to do
is to express p theta of x0

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:07.320
or log of p theta of x0.

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:15.150
So we've seen a few slides
ago that the way we obtain x0

00:54:15.150 --> 00:54:20.390
is sampling from a noise
distribution at time t.

00:54:20.390 --> 00:54:26.690
And then going step by step
from big t to big t minus 1,

00:54:26.690 --> 00:54:28.330
et cetera, up until 0.

00:54:31.310 --> 00:54:37.030
So in order to compute this
quantity, one idea for you

00:54:37.030 --> 00:54:41.510
could be to take the joint
probability distribution

00:54:41.510 --> 00:54:46.830
over all the time
steps and then sum

00:54:46.830 --> 00:54:52.070
across all these different
quantities except x0.

00:54:52.070 --> 00:54:56.470
A.K.A. you can just
marginalize, right?

00:54:56.470 --> 00:55:05.750
So one idea is to say that
the logarithm of p theta of x0

00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:13.230
is the logarithm of the
integral of p theta of x0

00:55:13.230 --> 00:55:18.102
and then x1 up to t.

00:55:18.102 --> 00:55:22.230
D of x1 to t.

00:55:22.230 --> 00:55:27.910
So this is just the
definition of marginalizing.

00:55:27.910 --> 00:55:31.110
So the problem with
this formulation is that

00:55:31.110 --> 00:55:34.830
it's actually something we
cannot compute because here

00:55:34.830 --> 00:55:41.630
we're saying let's marginalize--
let's sum across all possible

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:46.470
trajectories from
noise to clean image.

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:49.750
So the problem is
there are many of them.

00:55:49.750 --> 00:55:54.790
And these x's we saw
that these are vectors,

00:55:54.790 --> 00:55:57.550
and they can be pretty big.

00:55:57.550 --> 00:56:01.470
So long story short, is this is
in practice something that we

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:02.610
cannot compute.

00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:10.020
Which is the reason why we have
a recipe that we will go through

00:56:10.020 --> 00:56:14.140
in the next 33
minutes as to how we

00:56:14.140 --> 00:56:18.060
can compute that using
the forward process

00:56:18.060 --> 00:56:21.500
that we have defined.

00:56:21.500 --> 00:56:23.900
So in the interest
of time, I'm going

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:26.800
to skip some blackboard
derivation, which is fine.

00:56:26.800 --> 00:56:29.060
I'm just going to
give you the trick.

00:56:29.060 --> 00:56:32.140
If time permits we
can do a few ones.

00:56:32.140 --> 00:56:34.500
But the important
thing for you is

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:38.620
to just understand the
high-level strategy

00:56:38.620 --> 00:56:42.600
around obtaining the loss that
will be used to train our model.

00:56:45.380 --> 00:56:46.080
Sounds good?

00:56:48.660 --> 00:56:49.200
Cool.

00:56:49.200 --> 00:56:52.900
So let's start with
the first step.

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:57.580
So I told you that
this expression

00:56:57.580 --> 00:57:01.860
is something that is actually
not something we can compute

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:04.730
because it's just intractable.

00:57:04.730 --> 00:57:08.610
So a common trick
that people use

00:57:08.610 --> 00:57:13.570
is to find something
that is easy to compute

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:17.090
that is not necessarily
the quantity of interest,

00:57:17.090 --> 00:57:23.130
but maybe a bound that is
close to what you want.

00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:27.370
So here what you want is to
maximize that expression.

00:57:27.370 --> 00:57:29.810
So if you were to
maximize something

00:57:29.810 --> 00:57:33.690
that you know is lower than
that, so a lower bound,

00:57:33.690 --> 00:57:36.850
then it would also allow you to
gain some confidence that you're

00:57:36.850 --> 00:57:40.650
maximizing the right thing.

00:57:40.650 --> 00:57:44.330
So there is a very scary
formula on the slides

00:57:44.330 --> 00:57:48.570
which we'll go through right
now, which is the elbo.

00:57:48.570 --> 00:57:51.670
Have you heard of the
ELBO before this class?

00:57:51.670 --> 00:57:52.410
Yeah.

00:57:52.410 --> 00:57:55.450
ELBO stands for
evidence lower bound.

00:57:55.450 --> 00:57:58.610
And what that means
is it's an expression

00:57:58.610 --> 00:58:03.360
of a quantity that is a
lower bound of the quantity

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:06.840
that we care about.

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:08.500
And actually, this
is quite important,

00:58:08.500 --> 00:58:10.060
so I think I will derive it.

00:58:14.000 --> 00:58:17.320
So while I'm cleaning
the blackboards,

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:22.240
I just want to tell you
about what the trick is.

00:58:22.240 --> 00:58:28.080
The trick is that we
have our forward process

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:31.800
that we have defined, so q.

00:58:31.800 --> 00:58:37.640
And what we want to do is to
have q be in some way something

00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:40.080
that we appear.

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:46.760
Like in our equation, we
want to involve q in some way

00:58:46.760 --> 00:58:48.280
because what Q is.

00:58:48.280 --> 00:58:50.680
Q is this forward
process that we

00:58:50.680 --> 00:58:55.400
have defined when we do the
noise schedule this beta t alpha

00:58:55.400 --> 00:58:58.040
t, we know that.

00:58:58.040 --> 00:59:05.160
So what we want to do is to
express that as a function of q.

00:59:05.160 --> 00:59:11.600
So we go back with the
log of p theta of x0,

00:59:11.600 --> 00:59:17.540
which we said we tried to
marginalize across x1 to xt.

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:19.740
So I'm just going to
rewrite what we had above.

00:59:28.883 --> 00:59:30.800
And then what I'm going
to do, as I mentioned,

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:35.080
is have q appear.

00:59:35.080 --> 00:59:37.760
So very common trick.

00:59:37.760 --> 00:59:41.040
What I'm going to
do is to multiply

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:45.520
the numerator and the
denominator by that quantity.

00:59:45.520 --> 00:59:55.400
So I'm going to do
this one times q of--

00:59:55.400 --> 00:59:59.470
So given a clean image,
what is the trajectory

00:59:59.470 --> 01:00:01.450
that is given by q?

01:00:01.450 --> 01:00:06.490
So it's q of x1 to xt
given your clean image.

01:00:21.790 --> 01:00:22.290
OK.

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:24.470
Everyone is following me so far?

01:00:24.470 --> 01:00:27.270
Just a very regular trick.

01:00:27.270 --> 01:00:27.770
So OK.

01:00:27.770 --> 01:00:31.070
So where do we go from there?

01:00:31.070 --> 01:00:34.250
So I'm just going to
remove the log for now.

01:00:34.250 --> 01:00:37.230
So this is true.

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:42.350
So this expression
is actually nothing

01:00:42.350 --> 01:00:49.390
else than the expectation
of this quantity

01:00:49.390 --> 01:00:53.430
because this is as if you
were sampling x1 through T

01:00:53.430 --> 01:00:57.420
from this distribution.

01:00:57.420 --> 01:01:01.620
So it's as if you
were writing this.

01:01:15.043 --> 01:01:16.460
So it's as if you
were saying it's

01:01:16.460 --> 01:01:23.860
the expectation of this ratio,
which is p theta of x0 to t over

01:01:23.860 --> 01:01:34.020
q of x1 to t, given
x0, where x1 through t

01:01:34.020 --> 01:01:42.200
is something that you draw from
q of x1 through t given x0.

01:01:45.540 --> 01:01:48.377
OK I hope-- so don't
worry by the way.

01:01:48.377 --> 01:01:49.960
It's all going to
be in the recording.

01:01:49.960 --> 01:01:53.260
It's going to be much clearer in
case you cannot see very clear

01:01:53.260 --> 01:01:56.170
what I'm writing, but what I'm
writing is the expectation.

01:01:56.170 --> 01:01:59.850
This quantity is the
expectation of the ratio

01:01:59.850 --> 01:02:04.810
between p theta of
x0 to t over q of x1

01:02:04.810 --> 01:02:09.650
through T, given
x0 by sampling x1

01:02:09.650 --> 01:02:12.890
through t-- so the
trajectory through t

01:02:12.890 --> 01:02:17.410
via the forward process that
we know, which is given by x1

01:02:17.410 --> 01:02:18.930
through t given x0.

01:02:23.730 --> 01:02:27.330
So where do we go from here?

01:02:27.330 --> 01:02:31.950
So it turns out that there
is a very common trick--

01:02:36.370 --> 01:02:38.370
very common trick
out there that's

01:02:38.370 --> 01:02:41.850
called the Jensen
inequality where

01:02:41.850 --> 01:02:45.350
if you have a convex
or concave function,

01:02:45.350 --> 01:02:48.370
you have some very nice
property about something

01:02:48.370 --> 01:02:50.290
we're going to see.

01:02:50.290 --> 01:02:57.610
And in particular, in this
case, log is a concave function.

01:02:57.610 --> 01:03:03.490
So if you have the log
of some expectation,

01:03:03.490 --> 01:03:06.690
it's actually something
that is greater

01:03:06.690 --> 01:03:14.590
or equal than the
expectation of the log.

01:03:19.223 --> 01:03:20.890
I'm not going to write
this whole thing,

01:03:20.890 --> 01:03:25.010
but you know what I mean.

01:03:25.010 --> 01:03:29.130
So this is Jensen's inequality.

01:03:29.130 --> 01:03:30.010
Jensen.

01:03:33.530 --> 01:03:35.950
And this is exactly
what we get here.

01:03:39.010 --> 01:03:44.850
So one way we
progressed is instead

01:03:44.850 --> 01:03:48.290
of summing through all
possible trajectories,

01:03:48.290 --> 01:03:50.610
now what we're doing
is we're actually

01:03:50.610 --> 01:03:53.640
sampling from
trajectories that were

01:03:53.640 --> 01:04:00.920
drawing from the forward
process, which we know.

01:04:00.920 --> 01:04:03.560
And now you're wondering
why in the slide

01:04:03.560 --> 01:04:07.760
are we sampling x0
through T. But here I

01:04:07.760 --> 01:04:12.400
said x1 through t sample
from q of x1 through t

01:04:12.400 --> 01:04:15.760
given x0 is because if
you take the expectation

01:04:15.760 --> 01:04:20.120
of this whole
thing, then you take

01:04:20.120 --> 01:04:25.960
the expectation over
x0 of expectation of x1

01:04:25.960 --> 01:04:28.540
through t times that.

01:04:28.540 --> 01:04:33.258
So you use the chain
rule of probability

01:04:33.258 --> 01:04:34.300
that we saw right before.

01:04:34.300 --> 01:04:36.240
And it's exactly the same.

01:04:36.240 --> 01:04:40.360
Just one expectation of sampling
the whole joint probability

01:04:40.360 --> 01:04:45.180
distribution through
this q x0 through t.

01:04:48.000 --> 01:04:51.110
Does this make sense?

01:04:51.110 --> 01:04:56.910
So you may see this formula
and wonder, OK, it's

01:04:56.910 --> 01:05:00.470
still super complicated,
how can I compute that.

01:05:00.470 --> 01:05:03.330
Well, right now, you do not
know that it is tractable.

01:05:03.330 --> 01:05:04.870
I'm just telling you.

01:05:04.870 --> 01:05:07.710
But the intuition
here is we're actually

01:05:07.710 --> 01:05:11.110
not summing through all
possible trajectories.

01:05:11.110 --> 01:05:14.830
We're actually summing
through trajectories

01:05:14.830 --> 01:05:17.970
that we're sampling from
the forward process.

01:05:21.470 --> 01:05:23.430
Yeah?

01:05:23.430 --> 01:05:25.550
So now it's step number one.

01:05:25.550 --> 01:05:30.670
Step number 2 is
proving or showing

01:05:30.670 --> 01:05:34.190
that this is something
that you can compute.

01:05:34.190 --> 01:05:36.670
But before that,
I'm going to, again,

01:05:36.670 --> 01:05:40.670
give you some refresher of
a very important concept

01:05:40.670 --> 01:05:45.030
that is the KL divergence.

01:05:45.030 --> 01:05:48.310
Who has heard of KL divergence?

01:05:48.310 --> 01:05:49.750
Yeah, everyone?

01:05:49.750 --> 01:05:52.570
I'm just going to remind
you what KL divergence is.

01:05:52.570 --> 01:05:54.690
So if you take two
probability distributions--

01:05:54.690 --> 01:05:57.870
so let's say p and q--

01:05:57.870 --> 01:06:03.030
and you want to measure how
far apart they are, then

01:06:03.030 --> 01:06:09.670
there is some operator that is
here, the KL divergence, which

01:06:09.670 --> 01:06:14.310
quantifies how far
apart they are.

01:06:14.310 --> 01:06:26.780
And the way they do
that is by taking--

01:06:26.780 --> 01:06:28.530
maybe it's something
I treat on the slide.

01:06:28.530 --> 01:06:30.130
So I'm just going
to continue their.

01:06:30.130 --> 01:06:35.430
So by taking the sum of the log
of the ratio between these two

01:06:35.430 --> 01:06:42.630
probabilities weighted by the
probability distribution p.

01:06:42.630 --> 01:06:46.700
So in the discrete world,
it's going to be like this.

01:06:46.700 --> 01:06:48.980
But we're going to be mostly
in the continuous world.

01:06:48.980 --> 01:06:52.540
And in the continuous
world, the KL divergence

01:06:52.540 --> 01:06:55.940
is this, but in integral form.

01:06:55.940 --> 01:06:59.420
And it's the same
as saying you take

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:03.660
the expectation of the
logarithm of the ratio

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:07.320
if you were to assume that
x is sampled through p.

01:07:11.700 --> 01:07:12.780
Yeah?

01:07:12.780 --> 01:07:13.280
OK.

01:07:13.280 --> 01:07:20.900
So given that, we can prove
that the ELBO term that we saw--

01:07:20.900 --> 01:07:25.260
so you know this
lower bounds here,

01:07:25.260 --> 01:07:30.940
it can actually be
expressed with terms--

01:07:30.940 --> 01:07:33.420
so some terms that we
actually don't really care

01:07:33.420 --> 01:07:38.000
and we're just going to
ignore-- plus a term here,

01:07:38.000 --> 01:07:47.490
which is the KL divergence of q
of xt minus 1 given xt and x0.

01:07:47.490 --> 01:07:50.650
So it's the first distribution.

01:07:50.650 --> 01:07:52.970
And the second
distribution is actually

01:07:52.970 --> 01:07:58.650
the probability of having
the less noisy image

01:07:58.650 --> 01:08:06.130
xt minus 1 given xt, which is
the thing that we want to learn.

01:08:06.130 --> 01:08:10.170
So we don't have
time to derive this,

01:08:10.170 --> 01:08:15.190
but it's actually not that
complicated of a derivation.

01:08:15.190 --> 01:08:18.970
You would just take
this expectation of log,

01:08:18.970 --> 01:08:22.830
and then you would do some
manipulation here and there,

01:08:22.830 --> 01:08:25.930
and you can basically pop up
the KL divergence operator

01:08:25.930 --> 01:08:29.090
through that.

01:08:29.090 --> 01:08:32.130
So I think this one, I
will need you to trust me

01:08:32.130 --> 01:08:35.210
that this is equal to this.

01:08:35.210 --> 01:08:37.770
So why are we happy?

01:08:37.770 --> 01:08:43.000
The reason why we're
happy is because we

01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:48.620
have a clear comparison between
two probability distributions,

01:08:48.620 --> 01:08:52.000
one that we care about, and
another one that is potentially

01:08:52.000 --> 01:08:56.040
something that is tractable.

01:08:56.040 --> 01:09:00.760
And so just to tell you
what q of xt minus 1 given

01:09:00.760 --> 01:09:06.680
xt and x0 means, so it means
that if I were to give you

01:09:06.680 --> 01:09:13.859
a noisy image and a clean image,
my question for you would be,

01:09:13.859 --> 01:09:16.800
what would be the
less noisy image

01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:20.359
if I were to give you this tool.

01:09:20.359 --> 01:09:25.319
So the good thing is that at
training time, you know x0.

01:09:25.319 --> 01:09:27.620
So you can compute
this quantity.

01:09:30.800 --> 01:09:36.240
So a natural step
for us is now to show

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:41.399
that this quantity that I
mentioned is indeed tractable.

01:09:41.399 --> 01:09:50.520
So we're going to see what q of
xt minus 1 given xt and x0 is.

01:09:50.520 --> 01:09:55.840
So again, it is the
fact of wondering

01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:59.560
what is the distribution
of the less noisy image

01:09:59.560 --> 01:10:02.400
if I were to not only
give you the more noisy

01:10:02.400 --> 01:10:05.440
image, but also the clean
image, which is actually

01:10:05.440 --> 01:10:09.640
a lot of information.

01:10:09.640 --> 01:10:15.200
So you may remember, or you
may have heard of Bayes' rule.

01:10:15.200 --> 01:10:19.280
And it is something that we will
use, a trick we will use here.

01:10:19.280 --> 01:10:24.840
So using the notations
that you would commonly

01:10:24.840 --> 01:10:29.560
see and read and
hear out there, it's

01:10:29.560 --> 01:10:32.720
the fact of saying
that the posterior is

01:10:32.720 --> 01:10:35.760
equal to the likelihood
times the prior

01:10:35.760 --> 01:10:38.750
over the marginal likelihood.

01:10:38.750 --> 01:10:42.750
So in this case, it's P
of A given B equal to P

01:10:42.750 --> 01:10:49.030
of B given A times
P of A over P of B.

01:10:49.030 --> 01:10:53.550
And so knowing that, it's
actually quite easy to show

01:10:53.550 --> 01:10:56.090
that this thing is tractable.

01:10:56.090 --> 01:10:58.570
So this is probably
something we will derive.

01:11:03.910 --> 01:11:23.630
So here, so what we want is q
of xt minus 1 given xt and x0.

01:11:23.630 --> 01:11:30.750
So if we were to use
Bayes' rule with q

01:11:30.750 --> 01:11:36.940
of the conditional
probability based on x0 if you

01:11:36.940 --> 01:11:40.140
have this formula--

01:11:40.140 --> 01:11:43.540
so if we were to write
this by considering

01:11:43.540 --> 01:11:50.300
p equal to q conditional
on x0, what we would do

01:11:50.300 --> 01:11:54.700
is-- so what I'm doing is
just applying Bayes' rule.

01:11:54.700 --> 01:12:01.820
So it's equal to
q of xt given xt

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:20.760
minus 1 x0 times q of xt minus
1 x0 over q of xt given x0.

01:12:23.700 --> 01:12:25.260
You all agree?

01:12:25.260 --> 01:12:27.460
I'm just doing Bayes' rule.

01:12:27.460 --> 01:12:32.380
So it's something that I
have not told you so far.

01:12:32.380 --> 01:12:35.050
But q has a very
special property.

01:12:35.050 --> 01:12:41.210
Q is Markovian.

01:12:41.210 --> 01:12:44.050
So what does that mean?

01:12:44.050 --> 01:12:52.530
It means that q of xt,
given everything before--

01:12:52.530 --> 01:12:56.490
let's say before-- is
actually equal to q

01:12:56.490 --> 01:13:01.130
of xt given xt minus 1.

01:13:01.130 --> 01:13:06.830
In other words, you only need
to know about the previous step

01:13:06.830 --> 01:13:08.390
to know the current step.

01:13:08.390 --> 01:13:12.930
So it's called a Markov process.

01:13:12.930 --> 01:13:18.690
So this simplifies to q
of xt given xt minus 1.

01:13:18.690 --> 01:13:22.210
And then we saw before
that q of xt minus 1 given

01:13:22.210 --> 01:13:30.610
x0 and q of xt given x0 are
quantities that are tractable.

01:13:30.610 --> 01:13:32.190
They are this
normal distribution.

01:13:32.190 --> 01:13:34.250
Do you remember this
derivation I made with,

01:13:34.250 --> 01:13:39.050
OK, let's replace xt minus 1
as a function of xt minus 2

01:13:39.050 --> 01:13:40.350
until getting x0?

01:13:40.350 --> 01:13:42.010
Do you remember?

01:13:42.010 --> 01:13:45.350
So these two quantities,
they are tractable.

01:13:45.350 --> 01:13:48.730
This is tractable,
this is tractable.

01:13:48.730 --> 01:13:50.210
Actually, this one,
we don't really

01:13:50.210 --> 01:13:58.970
care because we want to compute
q of xt minus 1 given xt and x0.

01:13:58.970 --> 01:14:03.170
So here, we're only
varying xt minus 1,

01:14:03.170 --> 01:14:07.570
but q of xt given x0
everything is fixed.

01:14:07.570 --> 01:14:09.990
So you can interpret
that as a constant.

01:14:14.682 --> 01:14:17.650
And so what we end up with--

01:14:17.650 --> 01:14:21.150
I cannot do that.

01:14:21.150 --> 01:14:21.650
Yeah.

01:14:27.370 --> 01:14:29.960
Are you referring to this one?

01:14:29.960 --> 01:14:30.900
The left hand side.

01:14:35.000 --> 01:14:40.680
So here's the thing--

01:14:40.680 --> 01:14:45.840
in this derivation, the quantity
that we actually care about

01:14:45.840 --> 01:14:49.380
is q of xt minus 1
just given xt and x0.

01:14:52.120 --> 01:14:56.520
So this is just because of
the derivation of the elbow,

01:14:56.520 --> 01:14:59.560
the fact that we end up here.

01:14:59.560 --> 01:15:01.800
And that's just the
fact that we just

01:15:01.800 --> 01:15:05.280
are interested in what is
the distribution of the less

01:15:05.280 --> 01:15:08.280
noisy data if we what
the noisier one is

01:15:08.280 --> 01:15:10.040
and the clean image is.

01:15:10.040 --> 01:15:11.580
That's the thing
that we care about.

01:15:14.760 --> 01:15:25.040
And so here, what I want to
say is that this is something--

01:15:25.040 --> 01:15:32.590
so x of t minus 1,
knowing xt and x0,

01:15:32.590 --> 01:15:47.950
is proportional of q of xt,
given xt minus 1, times q of xt

01:15:47.950 --> 01:15:53.070
minus 1 given x0.

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:55.030
This is Gaussian.

01:15:55.030 --> 01:15:58.510
This is Gaussian.

01:15:58.510 --> 01:16:05.310
The product of probabilities
of Gaussian is a Gaussian.

01:16:05.310 --> 01:16:07.130
I'm not going to
derive everything,

01:16:07.130 --> 01:16:10.390
but please trust
me that the form

01:16:10.390 --> 01:16:13.670
of q of xt minus
1 given xt and x0

01:16:13.670 --> 01:16:16.610
is a normal distribution
in particular

01:16:16.610 --> 01:16:19.533
with the mean of this formula.

01:16:19.533 --> 01:16:20.950
I'm not going to
read it, but it's

01:16:20.950 --> 01:16:27.470
a function of the noisy image
and then the sampled noise

01:16:27.470 --> 01:16:29.730
that allows you to
go from x0 to xt.

01:16:33.110 --> 01:16:40.070
So this distribution is
exact, meaning we have not

01:16:40.070 --> 01:16:42.670
approximated anything.

01:16:42.670 --> 01:16:45.710
And this is something that
we know because we have

01:16:45.710 --> 01:16:47.808
defined our forward process.

01:16:47.808 --> 01:16:49.350
And here, we're
cheating a little bit

01:16:49.350 --> 01:16:53.590
because we're saying, what is
the less noisy distribution if I

01:16:53.590 --> 01:16:56.933
give you the more noisy one,
but also the clean image.

01:16:56.933 --> 01:16:59.350
So it's a little bit of cheating
because at training time,

01:16:59.350 --> 01:17:02.350
we know what x0 is.

01:17:02.350 --> 01:17:05.050
Of course, at inference
time, we do not.

01:17:07.910 --> 01:17:11.390
So know one term.

01:17:11.390 --> 01:17:16.150
The second term is this p
theta of xt minus 1 given xt.

01:17:16.150 --> 01:17:18.630
Now let's show that
it is tractable.

01:17:18.630 --> 01:17:21.270
Well, that one is going
to be easy because we

01:17:21.270 --> 01:17:25.100
can choose what p theta is.

01:17:25.100 --> 01:17:29.300
And turns out that
it's very fair for us

01:17:29.300 --> 01:17:32.300
to assume that it
is also a Gaussian

01:17:32.300 --> 01:17:37.860
just because the forward
process of xt minus 1 given xt

01:17:37.860 --> 01:17:41.460
is also approximately
a Gaussian.

01:17:41.460 --> 01:17:45.600
So justification for that is a
little bit more into the weeds.

01:17:45.600 --> 01:17:47.320
So I'm not going
to go into that.

01:17:47.320 --> 01:17:52.500
But all of that to
say that in this part,

01:17:52.500 --> 01:17:58.300
we're just making the assumption
that what we want to learn

01:17:58.300 --> 01:17:59.400
is a Gaussian.

01:18:03.546 --> 01:18:09.700
So what we end up with
is a KL divergence

01:18:09.700 --> 01:18:14.620
of a Gaussian with a Gaussian.

01:18:14.620 --> 01:18:16.120
And I'm not sure
if you remember,

01:18:16.120 --> 01:18:19.540
but early in the
lecture, I told you

01:18:19.540 --> 01:18:22.630
that we know the probability
density function of a Gaussian.

01:18:22.630 --> 01:18:26.310
We have the explicit formula.

01:18:26.310 --> 01:18:29.050
We do know.

01:18:29.050 --> 01:18:36.290
So if you replace these into
the KL divergence formula, which

01:18:36.290 --> 01:18:42.050
is this integral of log
of p over q and then p,

01:18:42.050 --> 01:18:49.290
then it turns out you have a
lot of things that simplify.

01:18:49.290 --> 01:18:54.130
And at the end of the
day, what you end up with

01:18:54.130 --> 01:19:01.330
is just the distance between the
noise that you want to predict

01:19:01.330 --> 01:19:05.330
and the noise that you sample
to go from the clean image

01:19:05.330 --> 01:19:07.490
to the noise image.

01:19:07.490 --> 01:19:09.310
Take the L2 distance of that.

01:19:13.250 --> 01:19:15.750
Is everyone clear
with that transition?

01:19:15.750 --> 01:19:19.820
So what we did was we
had that elbo elbow,

01:19:19.820 --> 01:19:23.400
which was equal to this
KL divergence term.

01:19:23.400 --> 01:19:26.600
And what we said
was, OK, looks good

01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:30.320
because p theta is what
we want to compute.

01:19:30.320 --> 01:19:33.260
And then this q thing is
something we have defined.

01:19:33.260 --> 01:19:37.840
So let's try to see
if it's tractable.

01:19:37.840 --> 01:19:41.040
And what we said
was, OK, let's see

01:19:41.040 --> 01:19:44.220
if q is tractable, q of xt
minus 1 given xt and x0.

01:19:44.220 --> 01:19:46.480
So we went into the math.

01:19:46.480 --> 01:19:49.440
We did the Bayes' rule.

01:19:49.440 --> 01:19:52.240
And then there is
this latter derivation

01:19:52.240 --> 01:19:55.440
around actually determining
the mean and the variance

01:19:55.440 --> 01:19:58.040
of that normal distribution
that I've not covered just

01:19:58.040 --> 01:19:59.840
because it's just too much.

01:19:59.840 --> 01:20:03.140
And it's OK to just assume
that if you do this at home,

01:20:03.140 --> 01:20:07.740
you end up with a normal
distribution of this mean.

01:20:10.760 --> 01:20:14.600
And then we also
showed that p theta

01:20:14.600 --> 01:20:16.960
is something that is tractable.

01:20:16.960 --> 01:20:18.720
And the way we have
shown that is just

01:20:18.720 --> 01:20:22.320
by assuming that
it is a Gaussian.

01:20:22.320 --> 01:20:27.480
So Gaussian of some mean
and then some variance.

01:20:27.480 --> 01:20:30.440
And at the end of
the day, we end up

01:20:30.440 --> 01:20:36.120
with a loss that is nothing
else than an L2 regression

01:20:36.120 --> 01:20:39.760
on the noise that we have
added to our clean image

01:20:39.760 --> 01:20:41.580
to obtain the noisy image.

01:20:44.800 --> 01:20:49.380
Do you agree that that loss
is looking pretty good?

01:20:52.160 --> 01:20:53.600
It's very tractable.

01:20:53.600 --> 01:20:56.360
You just have to
regress your noise

01:20:56.360 --> 01:21:00.160
on the noise from
the clean image

01:21:00.160 --> 01:21:03.800
to the noisy image
as a training loss.

01:21:03.800 --> 01:21:04.580
It's great.

01:21:12.040 --> 01:21:12.800
Oh, yeah.

01:21:12.800 --> 01:21:13.300
Thank you.

01:21:13.300 --> 01:21:15.500
So the question is,
what is epsilon theta.

01:21:15.500 --> 01:21:18.390
So I'm going to go
through each term.

01:21:18.390 --> 01:21:25.170
So here, what is shown on
the slide is loss of DDMP.

01:21:25.170 --> 01:21:29.590
So DDPM is this whole framework
of defining a forward process

01:21:29.590 --> 01:21:32.870
in order to determine
how you want

01:21:32.870 --> 01:21:37.990
to reverse the process, which
is why it's called LDPM.

01:21:37.990 --> 01:21:42.110
And what is on the right
hand side of the equal sign

01:21:42.110 --> 01:21:49.990
is an expectation over t that
you sample, which is the noise

01:21:49.990 --> 01:21:56.670
level if you want, x0, which is
clean images in your training

01:21:56.670 --> 01:22:02.910
set, and then epsilon is
random noise that you sample

01:22:02.910 --> 01:22:04.930
from a normal distribution.

01:22:04.930 --> 01:22:07.370
Sorry, standard
normal distribution,

01:22:07.370 --> 01:22:13.260
which is basically a Gaussian of
mean 0 and variance the identity

01:22:13.260 --> 01:22:14.500
matrix.

01:22:14.500 --> 01:22:20.860
So what is in the expectation
is on the one side,

01:22:20.860 --> 01:22:26.260
Epsilon theta, which is your
model predicting what noise

01:22:26.260 --> 01:22:29.740
was added, and then
on the right side

01:22:29.740 --> 01:22:32.020
is epsilon, which
is the actual noise

01:22:32.020 --> 01:22:35.380
that you added from
the clean image

01:22:35.380 --> 01:22:39.780
directly to the noisy image.

01:22:39.780 --> 01:22:41.360
And what is in the parentheses?

01:22:41.360 --> 01:22:45.860
So square root of alpha
t bar x0 plus square root

01:22:45.860 --> 01:22:53.580
of 1 minus alpha t bar
epsilon-- this is equal to xt.

01:22:53.580 --> 01:22:58.660
So it's the noisy image because
you can go from the clean image

01:22:58.660 --> 01:23:04.460
and epsilon directly
to the noisy image

01:23:04.460 --> 01:23:07.900
with using the identity
that we have seen.

01:23:07.900 --> 01:23:14.810
So in other words, it is saying,
if I give you the noisy image,

01:23:14.810 --> 01:23:18.370
please compute the
noise that was added

01:23:18.370 --> 01:23:22.010
and compare that to the
actual noise that was added.

01:23:22.010 --> 01:23:23.570
So one thing that
I will call out

01:23:23.570 --> 01:23:26.810
is there are actually
two arguments.

01:23:26.810 --> 01:23:30.930
You not only give the
model the noisy image,

01:23:30.930 --> 01:23:35.690
but you're also giving it the
information of how noisy it is.

01:23:35.690 --> 01:23:41.470
T. So here, t is
between 0 and big t.

01:23:41.470 --> 01:23:44.170
So if T is more
towards 0, it means

01:23:44.170 --> 01:23:46.690
you didn't add a lot of noise.

01:23:46.690 --> 01:23:50.170
And if t is more
towards big T, it

01:23:50.170 --> 01:23:53.810
means you've added
much more noise.

01:23:53.810 --> 01:23:56.230
So it just allows
the model to know,

01:23:56.230 --> 01:23:58.090
given this noisy
image, how noisy it is.

01:23:58.090 --> 01:24:04.690
So it can calibrate how much
noise to predict to remove.

01:24:04.690 --> 01:24:07.070
Does that make a bit more sense?

01:24:07.070 --> 01:24:07.570
Perfect.

01:24:07.570 --> 01:24:08.870
So yeah, but great question.

01:24:08.870 --> 01:24:13.290
And we're actually going to
go through exactly what that

01:24:13.290 --> 01:24:17.290
means in practice if we
want to train the model.

01:24:17.290 --> 01:24:23.350
But before we do that, any
other questions on the loss?

01:24:23.350 --> 01:24:23.850
Yeah.

01:24:26.970 --> 01:24:29.710
So the question is this
loss represents the elbow.

01:24:29.710 --> 01:24:34.170
So we are actually going
to see exactly how we

01:24:34.170 --> 01:24:38.510
went from the first step to
this step in the next slide.

01:24:38.510 --> 01:24:42.970
So I'm going to answer
a question right now.

01:24:42.970 --> 01:24:47.730
So apart from this any
other questions on this?

01:24:47.730 --> 01:24:48.682
We're good?

01:24:48.682 --> 01:24:50.390
So I'm going to answer
your question now.

01:24:50.390 --> 01:24:55.730
So do you remember
the strategy plan

01:24:55.730 --> 01:24:58.810
we had with these four steps.

01:24:58.810 --> 01:25:04.530
So we went from this maximum
likelihood estimation problem

01:25:04.530 --> 01:25:07.210
that we wanted to perform.

01:25:07.210 --> 01:25:10.260
And we said we
have a lower bound.

01:25:10.260 --> 01:25:14.320
So that's where the
elbow comes then

01:25:14.320 --> 01:25:18.360
from the elbow, which is a lower
bound of what we care about,

01:25:18.360 --> 01:25:22.520
what we said was actually
expand the terms.

01:25:22.520 --> 01:25:24.520
And we were like,
oh wow, we actually

01:25:24.520 --> 01:25:29.880
have a KL divergence between
two things that seem tractable.

01:25:29.880 --> 01:25:32.680
And then what we said
was, OK, so let's look

01:25:32.680 --> 01:25:34.340
at that KL divergence term.

01:25:34.340 --> 01:25:36.100
There are two parts to it.

01:25:36.100 --> 01:25:40.220
One is q of xt minus
1 given xt and x0.

01:25:40.220 --> 01:25:45.320
The other one is p theta
of xt minus 1 given xt.

01:25:45.320 --> 01:25:48.460
We saw that these two
terms were tractable.

01:25:48.460 --> 01:25:49.920
They were Gaussians.

01:25:49.920 --> 01:25:54.680
And we knew what the mean
of these two Gaussians were.

01:25:54.680 --> 01:25:58.420
And then what we said was
we have two Gaussians.

01:25:58.420 --> 01:26:01.240
We know their probability
density functions.

01:26:01.240 --> 01:26:04.560
We have a KL divergence of that.

01:26:04.560 --> 01:26:10.430
Then we can actually compute
this whole thing analytically

01:26:10.430 --> 01:26:17.270
and, as a result of that, end up
with a loss that's very simple.

01:26:17.270 --> 01:26:20.190
Now there is something
that I simplified

01:26:20.190 --> 01:26:23.830
that I've not told you yet,
but I'm going to tell you now.

01:26:23.830 --> 01:26:28.830
When you simplify this scale
divergence, what you actually

01:26:28.830 --> 01:26:32.590
get is the expectation
of this L2 regression

01:26:32.590 --> 01:26:36.030
times some coefficients.

01:26:36.030 --> 01:26:40.110
You can think of it as
the weight of the loss.

01:26:40.110 --> 01:26:44.550
And this is something that
you as the person who's

01:26:44.550 --> 01:26:47.350
training the model can choose.

01:26:47.350 --> 01:26:51.443
Now, the authors of DDPM,
they try different ways.

01:26:51.443 --> 01:26:52.610
They tried the actual thing.

01:26:52.610 --> 01:26:55.638
They tried without by
having a coefficient of 1.

01:26:55.638 --> 01:26:57.930
And they actually said, OK,
it's actually not that bad.

01:26:57.930 --> 01:26:59.950
You can actually
take equal to 1,

01:26:59.950 --> 01:27:03.990
but this is something that
you can actually choose.

01:27:03.990 --> 01:27:10.270
And this is how you end up
to this last expression.

01:27:10.270 --> 01:27:15.270
So let's see how we do
training and inference.

01:27:15.270 --> 01:27:17.710
So we see the strategy.

01:27:17.710 --> 01:27:21.130
So what you want to do
is to minimize that loss.

01:27:24.470 --> 01:27:27.370
So what you do is you
sample the amount.

01:27:27.370 --> 01:27:31.870
So first, the clean image
from your training set.

01:27:31.870 --> 01:27:36.430
You sample your noise
and then the time step.

01:27:36.430 --> 01:27:39.730
And in that way, you
constitute the noise image.

01:27:39.730 --> 01:27:42.710
So you can go from clean
image with the amount of noise

01:27:42.710 --> 01:27:45.670
up to the noised image.

01:27:45.670 --> 01:27:49.190
What you do is you
take that noised image,

01:27:49.190 --> 01:27:52.670
you take the noise level,
and then what you do

01:27:52.670 --> 01:27:55.030
is you try to predict
the noise that

01:27:55.030 --> 01:28:00.390
was added using your model,
which right now we don't know.

01:28:00.390 --> 01:28:03.260
It's just a black box.

01:28:03.260 --> 01:28:07.300
And you compute the loss and
you do your back propagation.

01:28:07.300 --> 01:28:10.100
So that's the training.

01:28:10.100 --> 01:28:14.520
And then the inference
is you sample your noise,

01:28:14.520 --> 01:28:17.900
which is super easy to do.

01:28:17.900 --> 01:28:23.300
And what you do is you go
step by step from noise

01:28:23.300 --> 01:28:25.680
to less noise to less
noise to less noise,

01:28:25.680 --> 01:28:31.380
up until arriving at t equals
0, which is clean image.

01:28:31.380 --> 01:28:36.020
And here, what you do is
you use the expression

01:28:36.020 --> 01:28:41.500
of p theta of xt minus
1 given xt, which

01:28:41.500 --> 01:28:44.300
is something we
have derived, which

01:28:44.300 --> 01:28:51.180
is a function of the noisy
image and the predicted noise

01:28:51.180 --> 01:28:53.660
that you can remove.

01:28:53.660 --> 01:28:55.440
And then you add some
noise on top of it

01:28:55.440 --> 01:29:02.210
because let's remember that p
theta of xt minus 1 given xt

01:29:02.210 --> 01:29:04.570
is a normal distribution.

01:29:04.570 --> 01:29:06.890
So the noise here
just corresponds

01:29:06.890 --> 01:29:11.570
to the standard deviation
of that distribution.

01:29:11.570 --> 01:29:15.090
And when you do that
from big T to 0,

01:29:15.090 --> 01:29:19.930
you then obtain
your clean image.

01:29:19.930 --> 01:29:20.610
Cool.

01:29:20.610 --> 01:29:22.090
I know this was a lot.

01:29:22.090 --> 01:29:24.130
I know this is lecture 1.

01:29:24.130 --> 01:29:27.370
I can tell you that this lecture
is probably the most difficult

01:29:27.370 --> 01:29:29.310
of the eight first lectures.

01:29:29.310 --> 01:29:33.070
So if you feel a little bit
like it's a lot to digest,

01:29:33.070 --> 01:29:34.330
it's normal.

01:29:34.330 --> 01:29:37.660
Hopefully we've tried to make
it a bit more digestible.

01:29:37.660 --> 01:29:38.910
Luckily, there is a recording.

01:29:38.910 --> 01:29:41.770
We have the notes,
we have everything.

01:29:41.770 --> 01:29:44.130
I mean, hopefully, if we
don't, just let us know.

01:29:44.130 --> 01:29:46.390
And with that, I'm going
to give it to Shervin.

01:29:49.890 --> 01:29:51.570
Thank you, Afshin.

01:29:51.570 --> 01:29:56.970
So now we saw how to generate a
clean image starting from noise.

01:29:56.970 --> 01:29:59.920
And what we're seeing
in this equation

01:29:59.920 --> 01:30:05.600
is that we need to repeat the
same procedure big T times.

01:30:05.600 --> 01:30:14.360
And the thing is this big
T is usually quite large.

01:30:14.360 --> 01:30:17.000
So it's the order of magnitude
of 1,000, for example,

01:30:17.000 --> 01:30:18.840
in the original paper.

01:30:18.840 --> 01:30:23.160
And let's say you
take the reference

01:30:23.160 --> 01:30:26.440
point of existing image
generation models before that,

01:30:26.440 --> 01:30:30.720
let's say VAEs or GANs, and
you compute the amount of time

01:30:30.720 --> 01:30:32.920
it takes to generate
a single image, which

01:30:32.920 --> 01:30:34.760
is the forward pass.

01:30:34.760 --> 01:30:37.280
So you have to do
that now t times.

01:30:37.280 --> 01:30:40.093
Let's say you had to do
it for 100 milliseconds.

01:30:40.093 --> 01:30:41.760
Now it might take the
order of magnitude

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:45.400
of minutes, which is not great.

01:30:45.400 --> 01:30:48.280
So one comment that we
have out of all of this

01:30:48.280 --> 01:30:51.760
is that we want to bring the
computational burden down

01:30:51.760 --> 01:30:54.680
at inference time.

01:30:54.680 --> 01:30:58.520
So what can we do
to make that happen?

01:30:58.520 --> 01:31:01.680
So I will first
show the equation

01:31:01.680 --> 01:31:06.560
that Afshin has shown, linking
the generation process from step

01:31:06.560 --> 01:31:12.000
t, which is a denoiser image,
to step t minus 1, which

01:31:12.000 --> 01:31:14.560
is the slightly
cleaner image, which

01:31:14.560 --> 01:31:20.000
was defined by that generation
process written on the slides.

01:31:20.000 --> 01:31:25.020
And we're going to try to see
how to make these steps shorter.

01:31:27.760 --> 01:31:29.720
So what can we do?

01:31:29.720 --> 01:31:34.880
One strategy could be
to try to skip steps.

01:31:34.880 --> 01:31:38.760
And the first thought
that comes to mind

01:31:38.760 --> 01:31:41.120
is to write down
equations and see

01:31:41.120 --> 01:31:46.200
if we can link a lower
step to a higher one

01:31:46.200 --> 01:31:48.760
and see if we can make a
connection between the two

01:31:48.760 --> 01:31:51.760
without going through
each of the steps.

01:31:51.760 --> 01:31:54.470
So let's start by writing
down the same equation

01:31:54.470 --> 01:32:00.190
for step t minus 1, and
then t minus 2, and so on.

01:32:00.190 --> 01:32:06.510
And you come up with a
relationship linking a lower

01:32:06.510 --> 01:32:11.030
step that you index by some
other sequence of numbers

01:32:11.030 --> 01:32:16.270
that you call tau I, which is
a sequence of numbers between 0

01:32:16.270 --> 01:32:20.670
and big T, where the gap
between consecutive numbers

01:32:20.670 --> 01:32:23.510
is bigger than 1
potentially, and you end up

01:32:23.510 --> 01:32:30.790
with a relationship that
involves step number tau I,

01:32:30.790 --> 01:32:38.150
which is the larger step number,
and some sum over some numbers

01:32:38.150 --> 01:32:41.630
and some sampled noise.

01:32:41.630 --> 01:32:44.790
And as you can see,
it hasn't brought us

01:32:44.790 --> 01:32:49.630
very far because you still have
quite a bit of model evaluations

01:32:49.630 --> 01:32:53.620
to make, which is exactly the
gap between the two steps.

01:32:53.620 --> 01:32:56.020
So that didn't work.

01:32:56.020 --> 01:32:58.380
What can we do next?

01:32:58.380 --> 01:33:01.700
So let's just keep that
sequence of numbers

01:33:01.700 --> 01:33:10.140
that represents sequences of
steps that skip some in between.

01:33:10.140 --> 01:33:14.140
One thing we could do is
try to guess a formula that

01:33:14.140 --> 01:33:16.580
links two different steps.

01:33:16.580 --> 01:33:20.480
And I'm showing you one
proposal of such a formula.

01:33:20.480 --> 01:33:24.900
And I'm not going to
derive it, but just

01:33:24.900 --> 01:33:28.160
going to verify that this makes
sense for two consecutive steps.

01:33:28.160 --> 01:33:34.300
So if you say tau I is
step t, and tau I minus 1

01:33:34.300 --> 01:33:39.100
is step t minus 1, when you
replace the numbers in there,

01:33:39.100 --> 01:33:42.940
you will find again the
relationship between step

01:33:42.940 --> 01:33:45.900
t minus 1 and t.

01:33:45.900 --> 01:33:50.090
And so let's suppose you
use such a relationship

01:33:50.090 --> 01:33:53.650
to link two different steps.

01:33:53.650 --> 01:33:56.690
Then what you
realize in practice

01:33:56.690 --> 01:34:01.530
is that as the amount
of stochasticity

01:34:01.530 --> 01:34:05.810
that you have within
steps increases,

01:34:05.810 --> 01:34:07.770
then your quality is
going to get worse

01:34:07.770 --> 01:34:15.970
as a function of the step size.

01:34:15.970 --> 01:34:17.110
So did that make sense.

01:34:17.110 --> 01:34:20.610
So by the way, the density
of formulas in this last part

01:34:20.610 --> 01:34:23.510
is slightly higher than
in the previous parts.

01:34:23.510 --> 01:34:26.490
And the goal here is
more to get the intuition

01:34:26.490 --> 01:34:30.330
of skipping steps and seeing
how it works more than looking

01:34:30.330 --> 01:34:32.610
like at each formula separately.

01:34:35.230 --> 01:34:38.950
So does that make sense so far?

01:34:43.690 --> 01:34:44.930
OK, great.

01:34:44.930 --> 01:34:47.170
So now we have
tried to skip steps

01:34:47.170 --> 01:34:50.310
and we have found a relationship
that could link the two.

01:34:50.310 --> 01:34:52.650
But we see that the
quality is not great

01:34:52.650 --> 01:34:55.730
when you take large step sizes.

01:34:55.730 --> 01:34:59.450
So what you're
trying to do now is

01:34:59.450 --> 01:35:02.610
trying to do this
large step skipping,

01:35:02.610 --> 01:35:08.170
but avoid that level of
stochasticity between steps.

01:35:08.170 --> 01:35:13.730
So in order to do so we go
back to the drawing board

01:35:13.730 --> 01:35:16.130
and look at the
derivation of the loss

01:35:16.130 --> 01:35:19.090
function that you had
in the case of DDPM.

01:35:19.090 --> 01:35:24.490
And one thing we realize
is that the a key part

01:35:24.490 --> 01:35:27.730
to get to the formulation
of the DDPM loss

01:35:27.730 --> 01:35:33.330
was the marginal
q of xt given x0.

01:35:33.330 --> 01:35:39.210
And we realize that when we do
modeling choices that reproduces

01:35:39.210 --> 01:35:47.840
that relationship on q, then
you can have a model that

01:35:47.840 --> 01:35:50.280
is based on the same loss.

01:35:50.280 --> 01:35:55.520
So this is one observation.

01:35:55.520 --> 01:35:58.240
So the problem
statement comes back

01:35:58.240 --> 01:36:02.320
to finding such a
q that reproduces

01:36:02.320 --> 01:36:05.120
these marginal probabilities.

01:36:05.120 --> 01:36:11.120
So with the same
x of xt given x0.

01:36:11.120 --> 01:36:15.360
And the second constraint
that we want to put ourselves

01:36:15.360 --> 01:36:19.280
is to have a generation
process that forgoes

01:36:19.280 --> 01:36:21.960
of that stochasticity
term in order

01:36:21.960 --> 01:36:28.840
to try to see if we can
mitigate the fact that quality

01:36:28.840 --> 01:36:32.120
of generation gets worse.

01:36:32.120 --> 01:36:33.800
This is the problem statement.

01:36:33.800 --> 01:36:37.920
I'm going to write this down
in more mathematical terms.

01:36:37.920 --> 01:36:44.550
So we want to find a familiar
function, let's say q sigma--

01:36:44.550 --> 01:36:47.710
and I'm going to say
what sigma is very soon

01:36:47.710 --> 01:36:51.910
and motivates why we are talking
about the family function here--

01:36:51.910 --> 01:36:57.270
such that we match the
marginals with respect

01:36:57.270 --> 01:37:00.830
to the DDPM forward process.

01:37:00.830 --> 01:37:03.630
And the second
condition where I said

01:37:03.630 --> 01:37:06.550
that we don't want any
stochasticity between two

01:37:06.550 --> 01:37:09.990
generation steps is
translated by the fact

01:37:09.990 --> 01:37:14.550
that we want p theta of the
least noisy image, given

01:37:14.550 --> 01:37:17.090
the noisy image to
be deterministic,

01:37:17.090 --> 01:37:21.510
meaning that once you have
xt, then your generation

01:37:21.510 --> 01:37:25.890
process gives you xt minus
1 as a one to one mapping,

01:37:25.890 --> 01:37:29.870
so there is no more noise term.

01:37:29.870 --> 01:37:31.930
Does everyone agree on that?

01:37:37.370 --> 01:37:39.510
So I'm going to throw
at you a formula

01:37:39.510 --> 01:37:43.310
and verify that this works.

01:37:43.310 --> 01:37:49.230
So this formula is
parameterized by a parameter

01:37:49.230 --> 01:37:55.150
that is called sigma here
that is a hyperparameter.

01:37:55.150 --> 01:38:00.750
And by reasoning with
the process of induction,

01:38:00.750 --> 01:38:06.990
we can verify that the
marginals q sigma of xt given x0

01:38:06.990 --> 01:38:10.830
matches the ones that we
had in the case of DDPM.

01:38:10.830 --> 01:38:16.110
And when you set
sigma to 0, you can

01:38:16.110 --> 01:38:23.870
see that this term becomes
just a mean with 0 variance.

01:38:23.870 --> 01:38:31.370
So you can link xt minus 1
to xt with no stochasticity.

01:38:35.790 --> 01:38:36.870
OK, great.

01:38:36.870 --> 01:38:40.500
And so this state
of mind and coming

01:38:40.500 --> 01:38:44.740
to the realization that you have
such a family of functions that

01:38:44.740 --> 01:38:48.500
verify that, and the fact that
this is attractive to seek

01:38:48.500 --> 01:38:51.900
is part of this
DDIM paper, where

01:38:51.900 --> 01:38:56.460
the only term that changes with
respect to the title of DDPM

01:38:56.460 --> 01:39:00.660
is this P, probabilistic,
to I, implicit.

01:39:00.660 --> 01:39:04.580
So the probabilistic part
of DPM translated the facts

01:39:04.580 --> 01:39:08.980
that when you generated a
clean image out of noise,

01:39:08.980 --> 01:39:14.620
you had this probabilistic
nature between two steps.

01:39:14.620 --> 01:39:17.700
But in this DDIM
formulation where

01:39:17.700 --> 01:39:20.900
you don't have stochasticity at
the generation step between two

01:39:20.900 --> 01:39:25.660
consecutive steps,
the stochastic part

01:39:25.660 --> 01:39:28.940
only comes when you
sample your initial noise.

01:39:28.940 --> 01:39:31.180
So your xT.

01:39:31.180 --> 01:39:35.600
And this is why we call the
diffusion process implicit

01:39:35.600 --> 01:39:38.490
because the probabilistic
part is not something

01:39:38.490 --> 01:39:40.270
that you see between
consecutive steps,

01:39:40.270 --> 01:39:44.750
but rather sets at the beginning
of the generation process.

01:39:44.750 --> 01:39:47.730
So I think there was a
question at the very beginning

01:39:47.730 --> 01:39:52.170
on why don't you start at
the same point every time.

01:39:52.170 --> 01:39:56.090
So if you were to
do that, then DDIM

01:39:56.090 --> 01:39:59.410
wouldn't have
worked because once

01:39:59.410 --> 01:40:02.790
you set your initial
point of noise,

01:40:02.790 --> 01:40:05.990
then you determine
deterministically your n image.

01:40:09.210 --> 01:40:15.050
And then in this case, one thing
we can do is, at each step t,

01:40:15.050 --> 01:40:21.050
we can approximate our belief
of what the clean image could

01:40:21.050 --> 01:40:21.630
look like.

01:40:21.630 --> 01:40:28.250
So if you remember, the marginal
distribution q of xt given x0

01:40:28.250 --> 01:40:33.090
gives you a relationship
between xt, x0, and the noise.

01:40:33.090 --> 01:40:36.040
And if you rearrange
the terms, then you

01:40:36.040 --> 01:40:40.920
can approximate
x0 at every step.

01:40:40.920 --> 01:40:46.880
This is just the rearrangement
of terms that proves that.

01:40:46.880 --> 01:40:52.840
And then you can plug-in
that approximation of x0

01:40:52.840 --> 01:40:55.880
into the formula that
we had just before

01:40:55.880 --> 01:41:01.040
to determine xt minus 1 by
weighting this term accordingly

01:41:01.040 --> 01:41:05.440
and adding to it
adequate noise weighted

01:41:05.440 --> 01:41:09.400
by the right coefficient
at step t minus 1.

01:41:09.400 --> 01:41:12.480
And as you can
notice here, there

01:41:12.480 --> 01:41:15.760
is no added stochasticity
by construction

01:41:15.760 --> 01:41:18.280
of this family of
forward processes

01:41:18.280 --> 01:41:22.760
Q sigma when sigma equals 0,
which is the property that we

01:41:22.760 --> 01:41:26.480
were after here.

01:41:26.480 --> 01:41:28.220
So does that make sense so far?

01:41:36.960 --> 01:41:39.340
So the question is where's
the skipping steps here.

01:41:39.340 --> 01:41:42.760
We have not skipped
any steps so far.

01:41:42.760 --> 01:41:46.020
We're just constructing
the generation process.

01:41:50.080 --> 01:41:51.360
OK, great.

01:41:51.360 --> 01:41:58.640
So if you recall, you had
these DDPM generation process

01:41:58.640 --> 01:42:01.360
that you had that
did not skip steps.

01:42:01.360 --> 01:42:04.840
So you had to go
one step at a time.

01:42:04.840 --> 01:42:12.200
And now you can adapt the
formula that we saw from step t

01:42:12.200 --> 01:42:13.960
to step t minus 1.

01:42:13.960 --> 01:42:17.680
But now between these
schedule of steps

01:42:17.680 --> 01:42:24.400
tau I's where at
step tau I minus 1,

01:42:24.400 --> 01:42:31.120
you predict your best guess at
what the clean image x0 is based

01:42:31.120 --> 01:42:36.190
on your step tau I, and you
approximate the noise that you

01:42:36.190 --> 01:42:37.330
need to remove.

01:42:37.330 --> 01:42:40.450
And you weigh each of
these terms at the step

01:42:40.450 --> 01:42:45.910
tau I minus 1, which is
how you get this formula.

01:42:45.910 --> 01:42:48.810
And here, we define
some concepts.

01:42:48.810 --> 01:42:53.350
So when I talked about
the sequence of steps

01:42:53.350 --> 01:43:02.430
tau I's, so the length
of that sequence is s.

01:43:02.430 --> 01:43:04.270
So we call it s.

01:43:04.270 --> 01:43:08.530
And t over s is the
effective speedup.

01:43:08.530 --> 01:43:11.790
So instead of spending t steps
to generate a new sample,

01:43:11.790 --> 01:43:15.470
you spend s steps.

01:43:15.470 --> 01:43:20.630
And with that in mind,
the value for the speedup

01:43:20.630 --> 01:43:26.270
that people typically
take is between 10 to 100.

01:43:26.270 --> 01:43:31.280
And as you can guess, it
doesn't come for free.

01:43:31.280 --> 01:43:34.700
So it's a trade off between
speed and actual quality

01:43:34.700 --> 01:43:36.140
that you get in the end.

01:43:36.140 --> 01:43:38.460
And the actual values
that people take

01:43:38.460 --> 01:43:44.140
are more on the order of
magnitude of 10 to 50.

01:43:44.140 --> 01:43:47.220
And here, I'm showing
what the authors

01:43:47.220 --> 01:43:50.700
showed in one of their
experiments, the evolution

01:43:50.700 --> 01:43:52.740
of one metric.

01:43:52.740 --> 01:43:56.720
So here, FID means Fréchet
Inception Distance,

01:43:56.720 --> 01:43:59.340
which is a metric that
we'll cover in lecture 7

01:43:59.340 --> 01:44:01.740
when we'll talk
about evaluations.

01:44:01.740 --> 01:44:04.080
So now I'm not going
to cover it in detail.

01:44:04.080 --> 01:44:07.900
Just going to tell you
that it translates how

01:44:07.900 --> 01:44:10.660
the generated images look like.

01:44:10.660 --> 01:44:14.680
And so the absolute values
don't necessarily mean much.

01:44:14.680 --> 01:44:17.740
But we can see the
impact on quality

01:44:17.740 --> 01:44:21.740
based on relative differences.

01:44:21.740 --> 01:44:26.580
And you can see that you can
speed up the process by taking s

01:44:26.580 --> 01:44:31.850
of size such that at that
20x, you're not too far from

01:44:31.850 --> 01:44:33.990
the baseline of not
skipping steps at all.

01:44:41.090 --> 01:44:45.330
So the discussion
here is so we realized

01:44:45.330 --> 01:44:49.890
that when you skipped steps,
the stochasticity that you

01:44:49.890 --> 01:44:52.410
had between two consecutive
steps that the generation

01:44:52.410 --> 01:44:59.370
process turned out to generate
worse estimates of what a lower

01:44:59.370 --> 01:45:04.290
step would be, which is why
removing it is better here.

01:45:04.290 --> 01:45:06.610
So here, we don't
show the comparison

01:45:06.610 --> 01:45:10.750
with methods that do not
remove the stochasticity,

01:45:10.750 --> 01:45:12.770
but the paper goes at
length into showing

01:45:12.770 --> 01:45:15.110
that when you do
have stochasticity,

01:45:15.110 --> 01:45:16.550
the results are much worse.

01:45:19.170 --> 01:45:22.690
So if I had to summarize the
thought process that we went

01:45:22.690 --> 01:45:28.170
through, we looked
at the DDPM loss,

01:45:28.170 --> 01:45:30.810
we looked at the
core ingredients

01:45:30.810 --> 01:45:36.330
of what got us there,
which was q of xt given x0.

01:45:36.330 --> 01:45:41.770
We chose q sigma that verified
the same relationship.

01:45:41.770 --> 01:45:45.930
And we made q sigma such
that it could be made--

01:45:45.930 --> 01:45:47.890
so we could have a
generation process p

01:45:47.890 --> 01:45:50.770
theta that is deterministic.

01:45:50.770 --> 01:45:53.450
And once we had this
deterministic generation

01:45:53.450 --> 01:45:55.650
process, so we call it DDIM.

01:45:55.650 --> 01:46:00.690
And then we skip steps,
and we get the transition

01:46:00.690 --> 01:46:06.210
from a generation process that
needs to go through T steps

01:46:06.210 --> 01:46:12.290
to one that only needs to
go through S steps, which

01:46:12.290 --> 01:46:14.650
is what we got in the end.

01:46:14.650 --> 01:46:18.570
And with that, thank you
very much for your attention

01:46:18.570 --> 01:46:20.760
and have a great weekend.

